  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- $T$ is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,\u27fa\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\"",
